<td id="S1.T1.2.14.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">second gradient of function <math id="S1.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.15" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.15.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.15.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">Hessian matrix</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.16" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.16.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.16.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">derivative with respect to time</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.17" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.17.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.17.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">gradient-tracking auxiliary variable at node <math id="S1.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.18" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">gradient-tracking rate</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.19" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.19.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">momentum rate</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.20" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.20.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.20.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">bound on <math id="S1.T1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.21" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.21.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{l},g_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{l},g_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.21.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">nonlinear mapping at the links/nodes</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.22" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.22.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.22.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">identity matrix of size <math id="S1.T1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.23" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.23.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.23.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">lower sector-bound</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.24" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.24.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.24.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">upper sector-bound</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.2.25" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S1.T1.2.25.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;"><math id="S1.T1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S1.T1.2.25.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.2pt;padding-right:4.2pt;">time index</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Terminology and Problem Statement</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The problem is to minimize the global objective defined as follows,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}f_{i}(\mathbf{x})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as the global state variable. This global cost function <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\cdot)\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\cdot)\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum of local objectives at some computing nodes/agents in the following form,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\frac{1}{m_{i}}\sum_{j=1}^{m_{i}}f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\frac{1}{m_{i}}\sum_{j=1}^{m_{i}}f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the cost associated with data point <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> at node <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of data points, and <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as the state variable at node <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Obviously, it is assumed that the optimization problem (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)-(<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) has a solution, which is denoted by <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in the rest of the paper.
In this paper, the following assumption holds for the cost functions <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmass1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ass">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmass1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmass1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmass1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmass1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmass1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define <math id="Thmass1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=\mbox{diag}[\nabla^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=\mbox{diag}[\nabla^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math> as the Hessian matrix. The cost functions satisfy the following</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H\preceq\zeta I_{mn}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H\preceq\zeta I_{mn},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})^{\top}H(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})\succ 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})^{\top}H(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})\succ 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmass1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmass1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The implication is that the local costs <math id="Thmass1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are not necessarily convex.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">An example non-convex model for local cost functions satisfying Assumption </span><a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="footnote1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> is given in Section </span><a href="#S4" title="4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="footnote1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span></span></span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The computing nodes (or agents) share data over a communication network <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> with adjacency matrix <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\gamma}=[w^{\gamma}_{ij}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\gamma}=[w^{\gamma}_{ij}]</annotation></semantics></math>. This network <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> might be time-varying with its topology changing based on a switching signal <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma:t\mapsto\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma:t\mapsto\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, where the set <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> includes all the possible topologies for <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. The entries of its
Laplacian matrix <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.EGx4.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.EGx4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{W}_{\gamma}(i,j)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}-\sum_{i=1}^{n}w^{\gamma}_{ij},&amp;i=j\\
w^{\gamma}_{ij},&amp;i\neq j.\end{array}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{W}_{\gamma}(i,j)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}-\sum_{i=1}^{n}w^{\gamma}_{ij},&amp;i=j\\
w^{\gamma}_{ij},&amp;i\neq j.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The following assumption holds for the network <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmass2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ass">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmass2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmass2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmass2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmass2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmass2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The directed/undirected network <math id="Thmass2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is WB, i.e., we have <math id="Thmass2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{W}_{\gamma}\mathbf{1}_{n}={W}_{\gamma}^{\top}\mathbf{1}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>γ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{W}_{\gamma}\mathbf{1}_{n}={W}_{\gamma}^{\top}\mathbf{1}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is also strongly-connected at all times.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="Thmlem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a><span id="Thmlem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="Thmlem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
For a strongly-connected WB network, its Laplacian <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> has one isolated zero eigenvalue, with the rest of the eigenvalues in the left-half-plane. Further, the zero eigenvalue is associated with left (and right) eigenvector <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>), i.e., <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}\overline{W}_{\gamma}=\mathbf{0}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}\overline{W}_{\gamma}=\mathbf{0}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W}_{\gamma}\mathbf{1}_{n}=\mathbf{0}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W}_{\gamma}\mathbf{1}_{n}=\mathbf{0}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that if <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is undirected, the eigenvalues of <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> are all real-valued <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_remov.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:349/202;" width="349" height="202" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An example graph under link failure: The graph on the left is both weight-balanced and weight-stochastic. After link failure, the graph on the right is still weight-balanced, but is not weight-stochastic. This example shows that weight-balanced design is more robust to link failure as compared to weight-stochastic design. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmrem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmrem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmrem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In contrast to the stochastic weight design for many existing distributed optimization algorithms, the weight-balanced design is more relaxed and easier to satisfy. In particular, for undirected cases, link failure or change in the topology of weight-stochastic networks necessarily requires redesign algorithms to reset the link weights and reassign stochasticity, see the works by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> for example. However, the weight-balanced condition is already satisfied for undirected networks, and there is no need for extra computations for assigning new weights to the network. This significantly reduces the computational complexity in terms of weight design as compared to the existing weight-stochastic algorithms. An illustrative example is given in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The other contribution in this work is to address sector-bound nonlinear models for information-sharing over the network <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows the real-world networking applications and distributed systems, in which the data is subject to (logarithmic) quantization or clipping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. In other words, the information received by the destination agent has typically gone through quantization and/or clipping due to limited communication resources. These techniques are often used to manage the representation and transmission of data efficiently and are used for optimizing bandwidth, storage, and processing capabilities while maintaining acceptable levels of accuracy and performance. The following assumption holds for these nonlinear mappings:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmass3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ass">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmass3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmass3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmass3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmass3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmass3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The nonlinear mapping <math id="Thmass3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> on the data-sharing channels is odd, sign-preserving, and monotonically non-decreasing. Such a nonlinear mapping satisfies the following sector-bound condition:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\kappa}\mathbf{x}\leq g(\mathbf{x})\leq\overline{\kappa}\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\kappa}\mathbf{x}\leq g(\mathbf{x})\leq\overline{\kappa}\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmass3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmass3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with <math id="Thmass3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmass3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> as lower and upper sector-bound rate.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">An example sector-bound nonlinear mapping satisfying Assumption <a href="#Thmass3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. As stated before, logarithmic quantization and (finite-domain) saturation are two nonlinear functions satisfying this condition.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_sector.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:250/220;" width="250" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>An example nonlinear function <math id="S2.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the sector-bound condition in Assumption <a href="#Thmass3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The sector-bound region is shown in grey colour.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we propose our distributed momentum-based algorithm to solve problem (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)-(<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). We propose a heavy-ball nonlinear perturbation-based gradient-tracking (<span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span>) dynamics as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-\beta)\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-\beta)\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{j}))-\alpha\mathbf{z}_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{j}))-\alpha\mathbf{z}_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{j}))+\partial_{t}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{j}))+\partial_{t}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where parameter <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> tunes the gradient-tracking (GT) and parameter <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\beta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\beta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> adds momentum to accelerate convergence.
The auxiliary variable <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is introduced to track the average of local gradients at neighbouring nodes. The nonlinear mapping <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{l}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{l}:\mathbb{R}^{m}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes non-ideal conditions such as quantization or clipping at the links. The momentum-based nature of <span id="S3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> can be seen by rewriting Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) in the following form,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{j}))-\alpha\mathbf{z}_{i}\underbrace{+\beta\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}_{\mbox{momentum}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>momentum</mtext></munder><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{j}))-\alpha\mathbf{z}_{i}\underbrace{+\beta\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}}_{\mbox{momentum}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the term <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> adds momentum to accelerate the solution towards the optimal value. The following comments are noteworthy regarding the momentum:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The heavy-ball momentum modifies the update rule by incorporating a term proportional to the derivative of the past iterates. This accelerates convergence by using <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inertia</span> to smooth updates and to overcome local curvature issues like ill-conditioning in the objective/cost function.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">However, improper choice of the momentum-rate <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> may cause oscillations around the stability point and, thus, divergence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The distributed nature plus GT adds coupling between <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> for stability. This is discussed later via detailed convergence analysis.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The solution is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. It should be noted that for different applications the data points are incorporated in the objective function <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and affect its gradient <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> which is involved in the auxiliary variable dynamics (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span><span id="algorithm1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> at node <math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. </figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.6.1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Given:</span> <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.6.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialization:</span> <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{i}(0)=\mathbf{0}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{i}(0)=\mathbf{0}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
          Every Node <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="algorithm1.6.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <em id="algorithm1.6.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;T_{end}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;T_{end}</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm1.6.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
          Finds <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\nabla}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\nabla}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
          Receives <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\mathcal{N}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
          Updates <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> via Eqs. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
          Shares updated states over the network <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.6.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.6.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return:</span> <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.6.9" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma states the GT property of the proposed dynamics to solve the optimization problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given Assumptions <a href="#Thmass2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the state update under the proposed dynamics (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) for initialization <math id="Thmlem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}(0)=\mathbf{0}_{nm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}(0)=\mathbf{0}_{nm}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the following:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{z}_{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),~\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=-\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{z}_{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),~\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=-\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Summing the dynamics (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) over all state nodes gives the following:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-\beta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-\beta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{j}))-\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{z}_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{j}))-\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{z}_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{j}))+\sum_{i=1}^{n}\partial_{t}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{j}))+\sum_{i=1}^{n}\partial_{t}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">From the WB condition in Assumption <a href="#Thmass2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and following the consensus nature of the proposed dynamics we have <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{j}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{x}_{j}))=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{j}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{i})-g_{l}(\mathbf{z}_{j}))=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-\beta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-\beta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{z}_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>α</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{z}_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\partial_{t}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\partial_{t}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">By setting initial values of <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}(0)=\mathbf{0}_{nm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}(0)=\mathbf{0}_{nm}</annotation></semantics></math> and simple integration, Eq. (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) results in <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}{\mathbf{z}}_{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}{\mathbf{z}}_{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), we have <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(1-\beta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=-\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>α</mi><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\beta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=-\alpha\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and the GT nature of the proposed dynamics straightly follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Further, the consensus nature of dynamics (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) leads to the equilibrium in the form <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}^{*}=\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes\overline{\mathbf{x}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}^{*}=\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes\overline{\mathbf{x}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
by setting <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=\mathbf{0}_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=\mathbf{0}_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}=\mathbf{0}_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}=\mathbf{0}_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}\otimes I_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}\otimes I_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\nabla}F(\mathbf{x}^{*})=\mathbf{0}_{m}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\nabla}F(\mathbf{x}^{*})=\mathbf{0}_{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}=\frac{d}{dt}\boldsymbol{\nabla}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.167em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\mathbf{z}}_{i}=\frac{d}{dt}\boldsymbol{\nabla}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\nabla}^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=\mathbf{0}_{m}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\nabla}^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}^{*})\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=\mathbf{0}_{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which leads to</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(1-\beta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=-\alpha(\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}\otimes I_{m})\boldsymbol{\nabla}F(\mathbf{x}^{*})=\mathbf{0}_{m}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-\beta)\sum_{i=1}^{n}\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}=-\alpha(\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}\otimes I_{m})\boldsymbol{\nabla}F(\mathbf{x}^{*})=\mathbf{0}_{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Therefore, <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{x}^{*};\mathbf{0}_{nm}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{x}^{*};\mathbf{0}_{nm}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the invariant equilibrium state of the dynamics (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
For mathematical analysis and proof of stability, we rewrite the dynamics (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) in compact form as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx13.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\dot{\mathbf{x}}\\
\dot{\mathbf{y}}\end{array}\right)=A_{g}(t,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\left(\begin{array}[]{c}{\mathbf{x}}\\
{\mathbf{y}}\end{array}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐱</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐲</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\dot{\mathbf{x}}\\
\dot{\mathbf{y}}\end{array}\right)=A_{g}(t,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)\left(\begin{array}[]{c}{\mathbf{x}}\\
{\mathbf{y}}\end{array}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.4" class="ltx_p">where system matrix <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}(t,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}(t,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by linearization at time <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx14.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\frac{1}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}\otimes I_{m})&amp;-\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}I_{mn}\\
\frac{H}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}\otimes I_{m})&amp;\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}\otimes I_{m}-\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}H\end{array}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mfrac><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\frac{1}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}\otimes I_{m})&amp;-\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}I_{mn}\\
\frac{H}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}\otimes I_{m})&amp;\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}\otimes I_{m}-\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}H\end{array}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:=\mbox{diag}[\nabla^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:=\mbox{diag}[\nabla^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})]</annotation></semantics></math>. In what follows, we explain how this equation is obtained.
Define <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi(t)=\mbox{diag}[\xi_{i}(t)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi(t)=\mbox{diag}[\xi_{i}(t)]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}(t)=\frac{g(\mathbf{x}_{i})}{\mathbf{x}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}(t)=\frac{g(\mathbf{x}_{i})}{\mathbf{x}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> with element-wise division. From the sector-bound property in (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), we have <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\kappa}\leq\xi_{i}(t)\leq\overline{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\kappa}\leq\xi_{i}(t)\leq\overline{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, in (<a href="#S3.EGx14" title="3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}=\overline{W}_{\gamma}\Xi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}=\overline{W}_{\gamma}\Xi(t)</annotation></semantics></math> which follows from <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{x}(t))=\Xi(t)\mathbf{x}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{x}(t))=\Xi(t)\mathbf{x}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> at every linearization point <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall from the definition of Laplacian matrix and consensus dynamics that <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(\mathbf{x}_{i}-\mathbf{x}_{j})=(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(\mathbf{x}_{i}-\mathbf{x}_{j})=(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, using (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) it is straightforward to get,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g(\mathbf{x}_{j}))=(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}\otimes I_{m})\mathbf{x}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>γ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\sum_{j=1}^{n}w_{ij}^{\gamma}(g(\mathbf{x}_{i})-g(\mathbf{x}_{j}))=(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}\otimes I_{m})\mathbf{x}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.6" class="ltx_p">One can derive similar equation for the auxiliary variable <math id="S3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> by further using <math id="S3.p5.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{d}{dt}\boldsymbol{\nabla}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\boldsymbol{\nabla}^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" lspace="0.167em" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{dt}\boldsymbol{\nabla}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\boldsymbol{\nabla}^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\dot{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It is known that the stability of the linearized system at every linearization point proves the stability of the original nonlinear system at all times <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>. Therefore, for proof of stability, we need to show that Eq. (<a href="#S3.EGx14" title="3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) has no eigenvalues in the right-half-plane; it only has one set of <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues at zero (with <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> as the dimension of <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>) to ensure reaching state-consensus among the agents and the rest of eigenvalues on the left-half-plane to ensure Hurwitz stability.
First, we rewrite the linearized dynamics (<a href="#S3.EGx13" title="3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{g}(t,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{g}(t,\alpha,\beta,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=A_{g}^{0}+\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}A," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=A_{g}^{0}+\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}A,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\kappa}A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\kappa}A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\preceq A_{g}^{0}\preceq\overline{\kappa}A^{0}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⪯</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\preceq A_{g}^{0}\preceq\overline{\kappa}A^{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{g}^{0}=\Xi(t)A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{g}^{0}=\Xi(t)A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle,~\underline{\kappa}I_{n}\preceq\Xi(t)\preceq\overline{\kappa}I_{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>⪯</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⪯</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle,~\underline{\kappa}I_{n}\preceq\Xi(t)\preceq\overline{\kappa}I_{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where matrix <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi(t)=\mbox{diag}[\xi_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>diag</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi(t)=\mbox{diag}[\xi_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the element-wise linear model of the given nonlinearity, and</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx16.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\frac{1}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})&amp;\mathbf{0}_{mn\times mn}\\
\frac{H}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})&amp;\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m}\end{array}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mfrac><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\frac{1}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})&amp;\mathbf{0}_{mn\times mn}\\
\frac{H}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})&amp;\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m}\end{array}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.EGx16.2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathbf{0}_{mn\times mn}&amp;-{I_{mn}}\\
{\mathbf{0}_{mn\times mn}}&amp;-H\end{array}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathbf{0}_{mn\times mn}&amp;-{I_{mn}}\\
{\mathbf{0}_{mn\times mn}}&amp;-H\end{array}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Based on this formulation, one can see the term <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}A</annotation></semantics></math> as a perturbation to the main term <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>). Moreover, due to sector-bound nonlinearity, one can derive a relation between <math id="S3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the spectrum of a matrix.
This is because <math id="S3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi(t)</annotation></semantics></math> (which models the nonlinearity at every linearization point) is a diagonal matrix and, thus, <math id="S3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{det}(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}-\lambda I_{2mn})=\mbox{det}(\overline{W}_{\gamma}-\lambda\Xi(t)^{-1}I_{2mn})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{det}(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g}-\lambda I_{2mn})=\mbox{det}(\overline{W}_{\gamma}-\lambda\Xi(t)^{-1}I_{2mn})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, at every linearization point, given that <math id="S3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\in\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\in\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma})</annotation></semantics></math>, it is straightforward to see that <math id="S3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\xi_{i}\in\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\xi_{i}\in\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma,g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Given the above preliminaries, in the rest of this section, we use perturbation theory at every linearization point along with Lyapunov theory to prove system stability. For this, first, recall the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="Thmlem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a><span id="Thmlem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="Thmlem3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Consider an <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-by-<math id="Thmlem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> parameter-dependent matrix <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given that <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{l&lt;n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{l&lt;n}</annotation></semantics></math> repetetive eigenvalues <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}=\ldots=\lambda_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}=\ldots=\lambda_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with (right and left) linearly independent unit eigenvectors <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{u}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{u}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> be the eigenvalue of <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\lambda_{i}}{d\alpha}|_{\alpha=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\lambda_{i}}{d\alpha}|_{\alpha=0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="Thmlem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th eigenvalue of</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx17.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{ccc}\mathbf{u}_{1}^{\top}P^{\prime}\mathbf{v}_{1}&amp;\ldots&amp;\mathbf{u}_{1}^{\top}P^{\prime}\mathbf{v}_{l}\\
&amp;\ddots&amp;\\
\mathbf{u}_{l}^{\top}P^{\prime}\mathbf{v}_{1}&amp;\ldots&amp;\mathbf{u}_{l}^{\top}P^{\prime}\mathbf{v}_{l}\end{array}\right),~P^{\prime}=\frac{dP(\alpha)}{d\alpha}|_{\alpha=0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\begin{array}[]{ccc}\mathbf{u}_{1}^{\top}P^{\prime}\mathbf{v}_{1}&amp;\ldots&amp;\mathbf{u}_{1}^{\top}P^{\prime}\mathbf{v}_{l}\\
&amp;\ddots&amp;\\
\mathbf{u}_{l}^{\top}P^{\prime}\mathbf{v}_{1}&amp;\ldots&amp;\mathbf{u}_{l}^{\top}P^{\prime}\mathbf{v}_{l}\end{array}\right),~P^{\prime}=\frac{dP(\alpha)}{d\alpha}|_{\alpha=0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmlem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="Thmlem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a><span id="Thmlem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Appendix]</span></cite><span id="Thmlem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Given a block-structured matrix <math id="Thmlem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in the form</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx18.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}D&amp;E\\
F&amp;G\end{array}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>F</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>G</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}D&amp;E\\
F&amp;G\end{array}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and having <math id="Thmlem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{det}(E)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{det}(E)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, determinant of <math id="Thmlem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{det}(M)=\mbox{det}(E)\mbox{det}(F-DE^{-1}G)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{det}(M)=\mbox{det}(E)\mbox{det}(F-DE^{-1}G).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">These lemmas are used in the following theorem to prove the main result on convergence and stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let Assumptions <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-<a href="#Thmass3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> hold. For <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\leq 1-\sqrt{\frac{{\alpha}\zeta}{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\leq 1-\sqrt{\frac{{\alpha}\zeta}{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\leq\frac{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|(1-\beta)^{2}}{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>ζ</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\leq\frac{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|(1-\beta)^{2}}{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as the largest non-zero eigenvalue of <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the compact system dynamics (<a href="#S3.EGx13" title="3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.EGx14" title="3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) has <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> zero eigenvalues <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> with the rest of its eigenvalues in the left-half-plane. Further, the proposed dynamics converges to the optimal point <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{x}^{*};\mathbf{0}_{nm}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>;</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{x}^{*};\mathbf{0}_{nm}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The proof analysis is divided into three steps. First, in Step I, we find the eigen-spectrum of <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, in Step II, we discuss how the eigen-spectrum of the matrix <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is affected as the perturbed version of <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for stability analysis. Finally, in Step III, we prove convergence by residual analysis and using the Lyapunov stability theorem.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step I:</span> Recall that, for the proposed nonlinear dynamics (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), the eigenvalues of <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> at every linearization point are related by</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\kappa}\sigma(A^{0})\leq\sigma(A^{0}_{g})\leq\overline{\kappa}\sigma(A^{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\kappa}\sigma(A^{0})\leq\sigma(A^{0}_{g})\leq\overline{\kappa}\sigma(A^{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is block triangular, we have <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(A^{0})=\sigma(\frac{1}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m}))\cup\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(A^{0})=\sigma(\frac{1}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m}))\cup\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the definition of the Laplacian matrix, <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\overline{W}_{\gamma}\otimes I_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> includes a set of <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> zero eigenvalues and the rest in the left-half-plane. Therefore, the eigenvalues of the matrix <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2n,j}\}\leq\ldots\leq\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{3,j}\}&lt;\lambda_{2,j}=\lambda_{1,j}=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2n,j}\}\leq\ldots\leq\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{3,j}\}&lt;\lambda_{2,j}=\lambda_{1,j}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">for <math id="S3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=\{1,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=\{1,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.p9.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step II:</span> From Eq. (<a href="#S3.E25" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>), one can see <math id="S3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(A_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(A_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> as the perturbed version of <math id="S3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(A_{g}^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(A_{g}^{0})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(A^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(A^{0})</annotation></semantics></math> via parameter <math id="S3.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. based on this, we check how the eigenvalues of <math id="S3.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, in particular zero eigenvalues <math id="S3.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, are affected by the perturbation <math id="S3.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}A</annotation></semantics></math>. This is done by using Lemma <a href="#Thmlem3" title="Lemma 3. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Noting that <math id="S3.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix, define the right eigenvectors of <math id="S3.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2,j}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx21.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V=[V_{1}~V_{2}]=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathbf{1}_{n}&amp;\mathbf{0}_{n}\\
\mathbf{0}_{n}&amp;\mathbf{1}_{n}\end{array}\right)\otimes I_{m}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V=[V_{1}~V_{2}]=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathbf{1}_{n}&amp;\mathbf{0}_{n}\\
\mathbf{0}_{n}&amp;\mathbf{1}_{n}\end{array}\right)\otimes I_{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.4.1" class="ltx_text">and the left eigenvectors as <math id="S3.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let define <math id="S3.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta:=\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta:=\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> and denote the perturbed eigenvalues as <math id="S3.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1,j}(\eta,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1,j}(\eta,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2,j}(\eta,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2,j}(\eta,t)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Eqs. (<a href="#S3.EGx14" title="3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.EGx17" title="Lemma 3. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we have <math id="S3.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{dA_{g}(\eta)}{d\eta}|_{\eta=0}=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{dA_{g}(\eta)}{d\eta}|_{\eta=0}=A</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx22.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V^{\top}AV=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathbf{0}_{m\times m}&amp;\mathbf{0}_{m\times m}\\
...&amp;-(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})^{\top}H(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})\end{array}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V^{\top}AV=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\mathbf{0}_{m\times m}&amp;\mathbf{0}_{m\times m}\\
...&amp;-(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})^{\top}H(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})\end{array}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.5.1" class="ltx_text">This is a block-triangular matrix with <math id="S3.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues at zero.
The sign of other <math id="S3.p9.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> eigenvalues is defined based on the following inequality,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left"><math id="S3.E46.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})^{\top}H(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\prec 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≺</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})^{\top}H(\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes I_{m})=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla^{2}f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})\prec 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.6.1" class="ltx_text">From Eq. <a href="#S3.E46" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#Thmlem3" title="Lemma 3. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we have <math id="S3.p9.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\frac{d\lambda_{1,j}}{d\eta}|_{\eta=0}=0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\frac{d\lambda_{1,j}}{d\eta}|_{\eta=0}=0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\frac{d\lambda_{2,j}}{d\eta}}|_{\eta=0}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\frac{d\lambda_{2,j}}{d\eta}}|_{\eta=0}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that by perturbing <math id="S3.p9.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{0}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{0}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.p9.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta A</annotation></semantics></math>, one set of <math id="S3.p9.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> zero eigenvalues remains at zero and one set of <math id="S3.p9.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> zero eigenvalues moves to the left-half-plane and toward stability.

<br class="ltx_break">Next, we check that other eigenvalues of <math id="S3.p9.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> do not move to the right half-plane due to perturbation. For notation simplicity and without loss of generality, we set <math id="S3.p9.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math> and drop the dependence on <math id="S3.p9.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma,g,t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma,g,t</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, one can find <math id="S3.p9.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(A_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(A_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> from Lemma <a href="#Thmlem4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> as</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{det}(\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}I_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{det}(\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}I_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big(\frac{H\overline{W}}{1-\beta}+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big(\frac{H\overline{W}}{1-\beta}+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1-\beta}{\alpha}((\frac{\overline{W}}{1-\beta}-\lambda I_{n})(\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n}-\frac{\alpha H}{1-\beta})\Big)=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1-\beta}{\alpha}((\frac{\overline{W}}{1-\beta}-\lambda I_{n})(\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n}-\frac{\alpha H}{1-\beta})\Big)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.7.1" class="ltx_text">We have <math id="S3.p9.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{det}(\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}I_{n})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{det}(\frac{\alpha}{1-\beta}I_{n})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, after some simplification and factorization, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big(\frac{H\overline{W}}{1-\beta}+\frac{1}{\alpha}((" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big(\frac{H\overline{W}}{1-\beta}+\frac{1}{\alpha}((</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\beta)I_{n})(\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\beta)I_{n})(\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\frac{\alpha H}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\beta)I_{n})\Big)=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\frac{\alpha H}{1-\beta}(\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\beta)I_{n})\Big)=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.8.1" class="ltx_text">which results in</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big((\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\beta)I_{n})(\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n})+\frac{\lambda\alpha H}{1-\beta}\Big)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big((\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\beta)I_{n})(\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n})+\frac{\lambda\alpha H}{1-\beta}\Big)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big((\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n})^{2}+\lambda\beta(\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n})+\frac{\lambda\alpha H}{1-\beta}\Big)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big((\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n})^{2}+\lambda\beta(\overline{W}-\lambda I_{n})+\frac{\lambda\alpha H}{1-\beta}\Big)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big((\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\frac{\beta}{2})I_{n})^{2}-\frac{\lambda^{2}\beta^{2}}{4}I_{n}+\frac{\lambda\alpha H}{1-\beta}\Big)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big((\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\frac{\beta}{2})I_{n})^{2}-\frac{\lambda^{2}\beta^{2}}{4}I_{n}+\frac{\lambda\alpha H}{1-\beta}\Big)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big((\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\frac{\beta}{2})(I_{n}\pm\underbrace{\sqrt{\frac{\frac{\beta^{2}}{4}-\frac{\alpha H}{(1-\beta)\lambda}}{(1-\frac{\beta}{2})^{2}}}}_{\mbox{perturbation term}})\Big)=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>det</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>±</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>perturbation term</mtext></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mbox{det}\Big((\overline{W}-\lambda(1-\frac{\beta}{2})(I_{n}\pm\underbrace{\sqrt{\frac{\frac{\beta^{2}}{4}-\frac{\alpha H}{(1-\beta)\lambda}}{(1-\frac{\beta}{2})^{2}}}}_{\mbox{perturbation term}})\Big)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.9.1" class="ltx_text">First, note that for <math id="S3.p9.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> all the above are zero, and <math id="S3.p9.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math> is one eigenvalue of the system.
Recall that all the eigenvalues of the Laplacian matrix <math id="S3.p9.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W}</annotation></semantics></math> are in the left-half-plane except one isolated zero eigenvalue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. For <math id="S3.p9.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math> the eigen-spectrum is the same as <math id="S3.p9.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\overline{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\overline{W})</annotation></semantics></math>. For large positive values of <math id="S3.p9.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, the perturbation term in (<a href="#S3.E49" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>) may lead the stable eigenvalues toward instability. Knowing that the system eigenvalues are continuous functions of the matrix entries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, we need to find the admissible range for <math id="S3.p9.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S3.p9.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) such that the perturbation term in (<a href="#S3.E49" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>) does not make the system unstable, i.e., to find the range <math id="S3.p9.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,\overline{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,\overline{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math> such that the non-zero eigenvalues remain in the left-half-plane. Therefore, for <math id="S3.p9.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to solve the following for given <math id="S3.p9.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{\alpha}=\argmin_{\alpha}\left|1-\sqrt{\frac{\frac{\beta^{2}}{4}-\frac{\alpha H}{(1-\beta)\lambda}}{(1-\frac{\beta}{2})^{2}}}\right|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><mi>α</mi></msub><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{\alpha}=\argmin_{\alpha}\left|1-\sqrt{\frac{\frac{\beta^{2}}{4}-\frac{\alpha H}{(1-\beta)\lambda}}{(1-\frac{\beta}{2})^{2}}}\right|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.10.1" class="ltx_text">The minimum value of the above is zero, for which the perturbation term needs to be equal to one. Thus, we set</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-\frac{\beta}{2})^{2}-\frac{\beta^{2}}{4}=\frac{\overline{\alpha}H}{(1-\beta)|\lambda|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-\frac{\beta}{2})^{2}-\frac{\beta^{2}}{4}=\frac{\overline{\alpha}H}{(1-\beta)|\lambda|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-\beta)=\frac{\overline{\alpha}H}{(1-\beta)|\lambda|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-\beta)=\frac{\overline{\alpha}H}{(1-\beta)|\lambda|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.11.1" class="ltx_text">Recall from Assumption <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that <math id="S3.p9.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\preceq\zeta I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\preceq\zeta I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, for <math id="S3.p9.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{W}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.p9.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|=\min_{i=1,\dots,n}|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{i}\}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|=\min_{i=1,\dots,n}|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{i}\}|</annotation></semantics></math> for non-zero eigenvalues.
Then, the maximum admissible value of <math id="S3.p9.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for stability satisfies,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{\alpha}\leq\frac{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|(1-\beta)^{2}}{\zeta}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>ζ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{\alpha}\leq\frac{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|(1-\beta)^{2}}{\zeta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.12.1" class="ltx_text">The above implies that for <math id="S3.p9.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;\overline{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;\overline{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, the nonzero eigenvalues remain in the left half-plane, and the proof of system stability follows. As a follow-up, for a given <math id="S3.p9.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the admissible range for <math id="S3.p9.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined as follows,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta\leq 1-\sqrt{\frac{{\alpha}\zeta}{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta\leq 1-\sqrt{\frac{{\alpha}\zeta}{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step III:<span id="S3.p9.13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Define the following residual vector for Lyapunov stability analysis:</span></span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx30.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\delta}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}{\mathbf{x}}\\
{\mathbf{y}}\end{array}\right)-\left(\begin{array}[]{c}{\mathbf{x}^{*}}\\
{\mathbf{0}_{nm}}\end{array}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐱</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐲</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\delta}=\left(\begin{array}[]{c}{\mathbf{x}}\\
{\mathbf{y}}\end{array}\right)-\left(\begin{array}[]{c}{\mathbf{x}^{*}}\\
{\mathbf{0}_{nm}}\end{array}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.14.1" class="ltx_text">and the following Lyapunov function,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{V}(\delta)=\frac{1}{2}\delta^{\top}\delta=\frac{1}{2}\lVert\delta\rVert_{2}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{V}(\delta)=\frac{1}{2}\delta^{\top}\delta=\frac{1}{2}\lVert\delta\rVert_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(58)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.15" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.15.1" class="ltx_text">This function takes positive values and for <math id="S3.p9.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\rightarrow\mathbf{0}_{2nm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\rightarrow\mathbf{0}_{2nm}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.p9.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., a positive-semi-definite function). Applying the proposed dynamics (<a href="#S3.EGx13" title="3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.EGx14" title="3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx32.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\delta}=A_{g}\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\mathbf{x}\\
\mathbf{y}\end{array}\right)-A_{g}\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\mathbf{x}^{*}\\
\mathbf{0}_{mn}\end{array}\right)=A_{g}\delta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐱</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>𝐲</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\delta}=A_{g}\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\mathbf{x}\\
\mathbf{y}\end{array}\right)-A_{g}\left(\begin{array}[]{c}\mathbf{x}^{*}\\
\mathbf{0}_{mn}\end{array}\right)=A_{g}\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.16" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.16.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, <math id="S3.p9.m62" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathcal{V}}={\delta}^{\top}\dot{\delta}=\delta^{\top}A_{g}{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathcal{V}}={\delta}^{\top}\dot{\delta}=\delta^{\top}A_{g}{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the results from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, Sections VIII-IX]</cite>, we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E63"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dot{\mathcal{V}}=\delta^{\top}A_{g}{\delta}\leq\operatorname{Re}\{{\lambda}_{2}\}\delta^{\top}\delta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dot{\mathcal{V}}=\delta^{\top}A_{g}{\delta}\leq\operatorname{Re}\{{\lambda}_{2}\}\delta^{\top}\delta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(63)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p9.17" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.17.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="S3.p9.m63" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lambda}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lambda}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is in the left-half-plane, which implies that <math id="S3.p9.m64" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dot{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is negative-definite for <math id="S3.p9.m65" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\neq\mathbf{0}_{2nm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\neq\mathbf{0}_{2nm}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, from Lyapunov stability theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> the residual <math id="S3.p9.m66" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> under the proposed dynamics converges to <math id="S3.p9.m67" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{0}_{2nm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{0}_{2nm}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., we have <math id="S3.p9.m68" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\rightarrow\mathbf{x}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\rightarrow\mathbf{x}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p9.m69" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\rightarrow\mathbf{0}_{nm}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\rightarrow\mathbf{0}_{nm}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This completes the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmrem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmrem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall that for the algorithm convergence and stability of the solution, from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E52" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) we need <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha}\leq\frac{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|(1-\beta)^{2}}{\zeta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>ζ</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha}\leq\frac{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|(1-\beta)^{2}}{\zeta}</annotation></semantics></math> or equivalently <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\leq 1-\sqrt{\frac{{\alpha}\zeta}{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\leq 1-\sqrt{\frac{{\alpha}\zeta}{|\operatorname{Re}\{\lambda_{2}\}|}}</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E53" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>). It is clear that there is a trade-off between <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> as the GT rate and <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> as the momentum rate. For large values of momentum (<math id="Thmrem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>), the bound on <math id="Thmrem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is tighter (<math id="Thmrem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\alpha}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\alpha}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore, one needs to balance between the momentum term and GT term in dynamics (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmrem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmrem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmrem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we only used Assumption <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> on the convexity of local costs. Eq. (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is used in (<a href="#S3.E52" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) and Eq. (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is used in (<a href="#S3.E46" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>). Therefore, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> converges even for non-convex local costs <math id="Thmrem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying Assumption <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. A numerical example is discussed in the next section.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Following Eq. (<a href="#S3.E52" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E53" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>), the algebraic connectivity (or Fiedler eigenvalue) of the network, denoted by <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, plays a key role in the gradient tracking and admissible momentum rate. Larger <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> allows for larger <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> rates. If the network topology is not locally known, one can use approximation methods to evaluate <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>; for example, for undirected networks, we have <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}\geq\frac{1}{nd}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}\geq\frac{1}{nd}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> as the network diameter. It is known that densely connected networks have larger algebraic connectivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>; this implies a larger admissible range of <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> rates for such networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmrem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmrem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmrem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmrem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmrem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmrem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The other factor impacting the convergence rate and optimality gap is the network topology. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> it is claimed that structured networks such as exponential graph topologies show faster convergence as compared to general unstructured ad-hoc networks or random networks. In the next section, it is explicitly shown that for networks of the same size, structured exponential graphs reach faster convergence than unstructured random Erdos-Renyi (ER) graph topologies.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In the proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, using perturbation theory for system stability allows to address switching networks. To be more specific, we only need that the perturbed eigenvalues remain in the left-half plane at all times, irrespective of the change in the network topology, and the proof is irrespective of the time-varying structure of the network as long as it is strongly-connected and WB at all times. Recall from Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> that the WB condition also allows for handling link failure (e.g., due to packet drops or congestion) in contrast to the existing weight-stochastic requirement in the literature, which requires redesigning the stochastic weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Numerical Simulations</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Locally Non-convex Cost</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we consider locally non-convex cost functions satisfying Assumption <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E64"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{i,j}(x_{i})=2x_{i}^{2}+\cos^{2}(x_{i})+a_{i,j}\sin(x_{i})+b_{i,j}x_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{i,j}(x_{i})=2x_{i}^{2}+\cos^{2}(x_{i})+a_{i,j}\sin(x_{i})+b_{i,j}x_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(64)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> set randomly in <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-5,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-5,5]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}a_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}a_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}b_{i,j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}b_{i,j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j},b_{i,j}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j},b_{i,j}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. For this locally non-convex cost function, we have</p>
<table id="S4.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E65"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla^{2}f_{i,j}(x_{i})=4-2\cos(2x_{i})-a_{i,j}\sin(x_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla^{2}f_{i,j}(x_{i})=4-2\cos(2x_{i})-a_{i,j}\sin(x_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(65)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">for which <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}f_{i,j}(x_{i})\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}f_{i,j}(x_{i})\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla^{2}f_{i,j}(x_{i})=4n-2\sum_{i=1}^{n}\cos(2x_{i})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}\nabla^{2}f_{i,j}(x_{i})=4n-2\sum_{i=1}^{n}\cos(2x_{i})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and, thus, satisfies Assumption <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Samples of these locally non-convex costs and the global cost as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathbf{x})=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf{x})=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{m}f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Locally Non-convex Cost ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_fi1.jpg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:175/131;" width="175" height="131" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_fi2.jpg" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:175/131;" width="175" height="131" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_fi3.jpg" id="S4.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:175/131;" width="175" height="131" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_F.jpg" id="S4.F3.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:175/131;" width="175" height="131" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The three figures on the top show the locally non-convex cost function <math id="S4.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i,j}(\mathbf{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E64" title="In 4.1 Locally Non-convex Cost ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">64</span></a>) at three sample computing nodes. The figure on the bottom shows the global cost function <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The parameters of <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm are set as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> for the GT rate, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.6</annotation></semantics></math> for the momentum rate, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> as the number of computing nodes. For this example, we consider log-quantization at the links defined as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E66"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{l}(z)=\mbox{sgn}(z)\exp\left(\rho\left[\dfrac{\log(|z|)}{\rho}\right]\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{l}(z)=\mbox{sgn}(z)\exp\left(\rho\left[\dfrac{\log(|z|)}{\rho}\right]\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(66)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> as rounding to the nearest integer and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{sgn}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sgn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{sgn}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as the sign function. For log-scale quantization (<a href="#S4.E66" title="In 4.1 Locally Non-convex Cost ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">66</span></a>) we have</p>
<table id="S4.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E67"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underline{\kappa}z=(1-\frac{\rho}{2})z\leq h_{l}(z)\leq(1+\frac{\rho}{2})z=\overline{\kappa}z," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underline{\kappa}z=(1-\frac{\rho}{2})z\leq h_{l}(z)\leq(1+\frac{\rho}{2})z=\overline{\kappa}z,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(67)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which satisfies Assumption <a href="#Thmass3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
For this simulation we set <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{1}{64}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>64</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{1}{64}</annotation></semantics></math>. Two networks are considered: a structured exponential network and an unstructured random ER network. The link weights are set randomly such that they satisfy the WB condition in Assumption <a href="#Thmass2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. To solve the distributed optimization problem, we use Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and the proposed dynamics (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)-(<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). While our algorithm is presented in a continuous-time framework for analytical clarity and to apply tools from continuous-time dynamical systems theory, for practical implementation, we use a discrete-time approximation using the Euler approximation technique. The simulation is shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Locally Non-convex Cost ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. As stated in Remark <a href="#Thmrem4" title="Remark 4. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the structured network of the same size and connectivity reaches faster convergence with a low optimality gap. It is clear from the figure that the solution converges in the presence of logarithmically quantized information exchange, and the rate of convergence can be improved by increasing the momentum parameter <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> (within the admissible range).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_nonconv0.jpg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_nonconv.jpg" id="S4.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The convergence of the <span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm under logarithmically quantized data exchange over structured and unstructured networks with <math id="S4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> (Left) and <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.6</annotation></semantics></math> (Right). Clearly, structured networks lead to faster convergence. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we redo the simulation for different values of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> by fixing the value of one and changing the other one. This illustrates how the change in one of the parameters <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> affects the convergence rate. Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Locally Non-convex Cost ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> presents the simulation results. As it is clear from the figure, increasing either of the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> parameters accelerates the convergence. However, the rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy Eq. (<a href="#S3.E52" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E53" title="In Proof. ‣ 3 Momentum-based Distributed Algorithm and Convergence Results ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_beta.jpg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_alpha.jpg" id="S4.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>The convergence rate of the <span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm for different values of <math id="S4.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>: (Left) changing <math id="S4.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and setting fixed-value <math id="S4.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> (Right) changing <math id="S4.F5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and setting fixed-value <math id="S4.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Distributed SVM</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we consider a set of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=100</annotation></semantics></math> data points distributed between a group of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math> computing nodes/agents. The network is considered as a simple cycle with link weights equal to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies the WB condition in Assumption <a href="#Thmass2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Every node has access to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the overall data points (randomly chosen). The goal is to classify these data points locally using a distributed support-vector-machine (SVM). The data points are not separable linearly via the SVM hyperplane and, thus, a Kernel mapping is used to transform the data points into a linearly separable form as shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Distributed SVM ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_data0.jpg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_data1.jpg" id="S4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>(Left) The data points given in 2D space are not linearly seperable. (Right) The data points are transformed into 3D space with proper Kernel mapping and are linearly separable via the SVM hyperplane in this new form. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The following cost function (known as the logarithmic hinge loss) is used to classify the data points:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E68"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}+C\sum_{j=1}^{m_{i}}\tfrac{1}{\mu}\log(1+\exp(\mu y_{i}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>μ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}+C\sum_{j=1}^{m_{i}}\tfrac{1}{\mu}\log(1+\exp(\mu y_{i})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(68)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E69"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}^{\top};\nu_{i}]}\in\mathbb{R}^{4},~~{y_{i}=1-l_{j}(\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}^{\top}\phi(\boldsymbol{\chi}^{i}_{j})-\nu_{i})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}^{\top};\nu_{i}]}\in\mathbb{R}^{4},~~{y_{i}=1-l_{j}(\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i}^{\top}\phi(\boldsymbol{\chi}^{i}_{j})-\nu_{i})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(69)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i};\nu_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\boldsymbol{\omega}_{i};\nu_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the SVM classifier parameters at node <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}^{i}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}^{i}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the coordinates of the data point <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> given to node <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the label of this data point, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Kernel mapping. The objective function is convex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> and for finite number of bounded-value data points satisfies Assumption <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The parameters of the hing loss function are set as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and the parameter of the <span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm is set as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{1}{128}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>128</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{1}{128}</annotation></semantics></math> as the level of log-scale quantization. Recall from the previous subsection that log-scale quantization is sector-bound nonlinear and satisfies Assumption <a href="#Thmass3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The time evolution of the hyperline parameters is shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.2 Distributed SVM ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. The figure clearly shows that the hyperplane parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i},\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i},\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> converge to the optimal value.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_Fsvm.jpg" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_dFsvm.jpg" id="S4.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_omega.jpg" id="S4.F7.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_nu.jpg" id="S4.F7.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>The time evolution of the SVM parameters for the data-set given in Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Distributed SVM ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. (TopLeft) The loss function decreases over time. (TopRight) The auxiliary variable <math id="S4.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (which tracks the sum of the gradients) at all nodes converges to zero. (Bottom) The hyperplane parameters <math id="S4.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i},\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i},\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> converge to the optimal value denoted by the black line. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Distributed Linear Regression</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a set of data points <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{i},y_{i}]\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{i},y_{i}]\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.3 Distributed Linear Regression ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>-TopLeft. These data are distributed among <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math> nodes to locally find the regressor line in the form <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{i}-\nu=y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{i}-\nu=y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.  The objective function for this problem is given as,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx39" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E70"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{m_{i}}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{i}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}^{i}_{j}-\nu_{i}-y_{j})^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{i}(\mathbf{x}_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{m_{i}}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{i}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}^{i}_{j}-\nu_{i}-y_{j})^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(70)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E71"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\boldsymbol{\beta}_{i};\nu_{i}]}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\boldsymbol{\beta}_{i};\nu_{i}]}\in\mathbb{R}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(71)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\boldsymbol{\beta}_{i};\nu_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}=[\boldsymbol{\beta}_{i};\nu_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the regressor line parameters at node <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\boldsymbol{\chi}^{i}_{j},y_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\boldsymbol{\chi}^{i}_{j},y_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the coordinates of the data point <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> at node <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. This objective function is quadratic and therefore satisfies Assumption <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
For this example, we consider clipping at the links defined as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx40" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.EGx40.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.EGx40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{l}(z)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}z,&amp;\text{if}~-\rho\leq z\leq\rho,\\
\rho,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{array}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.552em">−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{l}(z)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}z,&amp;\text{if}~-\rho\leq z\leq\rho,\\
\rho,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with the clipping level <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=10</annotation></semantics></math>. Given that <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\min}\leq z\leq z_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\min}\leq z\leq z_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sector bounds for this nonlinear mapping are defined as <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\rho}{\max\{|z_{\max}|,|z_{\min}|\}}z\leq h_{l}(z)\leq z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\rho}{\max\{|z_{\max}|,|z_{\min}|\}}z\leq h_{l}(z)\leq z</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\kappa}=\frac{\rho}{\max\{|z_{\max}|,|z_{\min}|\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\kappa}=\frac{\rho}{\max\{|z_{\max}|,|z_{\min}|\}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\kappa}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\kappa}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) satisfying Assumption <a href="#Thmass3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The network of agents is a time-varying random ER graph with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40\%</annotation></semantics></math> linking probability, where the topology changes every <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0015</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0015</annotation></semantics></math> sec. The link weights of ER graphs are designed to be WB satisfying Assumption <a href="#Thmass2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The time-evolution of the regression cost and the regression parameters at all nodes under the <span id="S4.SS3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm (with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.4</annotation></semantics></math>) are shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.3 Distributed Linear Regression ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Clearly, the cost function decreases over time, and the regressor line parameters at all computing nodes reach consensus on the optimal value.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_lr_data.jpg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_lr_cost.jpg" id="S4.F8.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_lr_beta.jpg" id="S4.F8.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_lr_nu.jpg" id="S4.F8.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:225/169;" width="225" height="169" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>The data points and the regressor line parameters versus time under <span id="S4.F8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm. (TopLeft) The data points and the optimal regressor line using centralized optimization. (TopRight) The regression cost function decreases over time towards the optimal value. (Bottom) The regressor line parameters <math id="S4.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}_{i},\nu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}_{i},\nu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> reach consensus and converge to the optimal value. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Distributed Logistic Regression: Comparison with the Literature</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we consider a real data-set example and compare our solution with the existing literature. For the simulation, <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=12000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=12000</annotation></semantics></math> labelled image data are randomly selected from the MNIST dataset. The purpose is to classify these images using logistic regression with a convex regularizer, where the data is distributed among <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> nodes. The objective function is defined as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx41" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E74"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{b},c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{b},c}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E74.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F(\mathbf{b},c)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}f_{i}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F(\mathbf{b},c)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}f_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(74)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with every node <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> accessing a batch of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}=\frac{N}{n}=750" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>750</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}=\frac{N}{n}=750</annotation></semantics></math> sample images. Then, every node locally minimizes the following training objective:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E75"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{i}(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{m_{i}}\sum_{j=1}^{m_{i}}\ln(1+\exp(-(\mathbf{b}^{\top}\mathcal{X}_{i,j}+c)\mathcal{Y}_{i,j}))+\frac{\theta}{2}\|\mathbf{b}\|_{2}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{i}(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{m_{i}}\sum_{j=1}^{m_{i}}\ln(1+\exp(-(\mathbf{b}^{\top}\mathcal{X}_{i,j}+c)\mathcal{Y}_{i,j}))+\frac{\theta}{2}\|\mathbf{b}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_left_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(75)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which is smooth because of the addition of the regularizer <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample at node <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a tuple <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}_{i,j},\mathcal{Y}_{i,j}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{784}\times\{+1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>784</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}_{i,j},\mathcal{Y}_{i,j}}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{784}\times\{+1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b},c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b},c</annotation></semantics></math> are the regression objective parameters (or the parameters of the separating hyperplane). This objective function is convex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> and for finite number of bounded-value data points clearly satisfies Assumption <a href="#Thmass1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We run the algorithms over an exponential graph as a structured network to better show the convergence results. The link weights are designed WB to satisfy Assumption <a href="#Thmass2" title="Assumption 2. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Applying log-quantized data exchange with <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\frac{1}{128}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>128</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\frac{1}{128}</annotation></semantics></math> the dynamics also satisfies Assumption <a href="#Thmass3" title="Assumption 3. ‣ 2 Terminology and Problem Statement ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We compare our distributed <span id="S4.SS4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm with <span id="S4.SS4.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GP</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SGP</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">S-ADDOPT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ADDOPT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S4.SS4.p1.3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PushSAGA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> algorithms. For all algorithms, the step-sizes are hand-tuned for best performance and for <span id="S4.SS4.p1.3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm we have <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.
The optimality gaps (or the optimization residual) are compared
in Fig. <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.4 Distributed Logistic Regression: Comparison with the Literature ‣ 4 Numerical Simulations ‣ Momentum-based Accelerated Algorithm for Distributed Optimization under Sector-Bound Nonlinearity" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, which clearly shows the accelerated convergence of our algorithm as compared with the existing results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22855v1/fig_MNIST.jpg" id="S4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:349/275;" width="349" height="275" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>This figure shows the logistic-regression convergence on the MNIST data-set for the proposed <span id="S4.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HBNP-GT</span> algorithm as compared with some existing works .</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Concluding Remarks</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper provides an accelerated momentum-based distributed algorithm for locally non-convex optimization over WB and possibly time-varying directed networks. The proposed method advances the state-of-the-art by addressing log-scale quantization, clipping, and general nonlinear sector-bound functions on data-sharing channels.
Our analytical and simulation results clearly show that the performance of the proposed algorithm does not degrade in the presence of these sector-bound nonlinearities. Different machine-learning applications are simulated to verify the results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Future Research</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The perturbation-based eigen-spectrum analysis in this work can be used to address (i) time-varying setups and (ii) general nonlinear sector-bound functions (e.g., log-scale quantization and clipping) for different distributed optimization, machine learning, and resource allocation applications. These nonlinear and time-varying models practically exist in most real-world distributed setups and may lead to sub-optimal convergence and even instability. As another direction of future research, one can extend the solution by applying Nesterov’s momentum technique. Unlike the heavy-ball method in this paper, which uses a direct momentum term from the previous step, Nesterov’s method incorporates a look-ahead gradient evaluation, which leads to improved convergence rate.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
